{-| The proto3 JSON helpers that code generated by elm-connect calls: how a
generated module reads and writes each message, field by field. They are
documented for reading generated code, and for writing a module in its form
by hand. A program writes a message's JSON with
[`Proto.Json.Encode`](Proto-Json-Encode) and reads a scalar or well-known
value with [`Proto.Json.Decode`](Proto-Json-Decode).

A decoder is named for what it reads (`int64Dict`), a value's encoder is
`encode` and that name (`encodeInt64`), and a field's encoder, which gives
`Nothing` to leave the field out, ends in `Field` (`repeatedField`).


# Fields

@docs field, fieldEither, valueField, valueFieldEither, emptyMessage


# Messages

Generated decoders check once that the JSON is an object and then read each
field without checking again, which makes a message decode faster than a
pipeline of [`field`](#field) steps, each checking for itself.

@docs message, messageField, messageFieldEither, messageValueField, messageValueFieldEither, nestedMessageValueField, nestedMessageValueFieldEither


# Encoding fields

A generated encoder leaves out every field at its proto3 default, as protobuf's
own JSON writers do: a field without presence at its zero value, a field with
presence (`Maybe`) when `Nothing`, a repeated or map field when empty. A parser
reads a missing field as its default, so nothing is lost.

    encodeNote : Note -> Proto.Json.Encode.Encoder x
    encodeNote value =
        Proto.Json.Encode.succeed
            (objectOfFields
                [ ( "title", implicitField "" Json.Encode.string value.title )
                , ( "score", implicitFloatField value.score )
                , ( "author", totalOptionalField encodeAuthor value.author )
                , ( "tags", repeatedField Json.Encode.string value.tags )
                ]
            )

A field holding a message that holds nothing a write can refuse unwraps that
message's encoder with
[`Proto.Json.Encode.encodeTotal`](Proto-Json-Encode#encodeTotal), through the
`total` helpers.

A message holding a value a write can refuse (an `Any`, a `Timestamp`,
`Duration`, `FieldMask`, `Struct`, `ListValue` or `Value`, or a message that
holds one) is built with
[`Proto.Json.Encode.object`](Proto-Json-Encode#object) from its fields in
declaration order, in runs: those that cannot be refused as the helpers here
give them, and those that can as a
[`Proto.Json.Encode.Field`](Proto-Json-Encode#Field) each.

    encodeEvent : Event -> Proto.Json.Encode.Encoder Proto.Error.Error
    encodeEvent value =
        Proto.Json.Encode.object
            [ Proto.Json.Encode.plainFields [ ( "title", implicitField "" Json.Encode.string value.title ) ]
            , Proto.Json.Encode.checkedFields
                [ ( "at", Proto.Json.Encode.optional Proto.Json.Encode.timestamp value.at )
                , ( "payload", Proto.Json.Encode.optional Proto.Any.encode value.payload )
                ]
            ]

@docs objectOfFields
@docs implicitField, implicitFloatField, implicitBytesField, repeatedField, dictField, boolMapField
@docs totalOptionalField, totalRepeatedField, totalDictField, totalBoolMapField


# Messages that nest

A message that nests is read at most 100 levels deep. It nests when, through
its fields at any depth, it reaches a message on a cycle of references, which
can nest without bound, a `Value`, `Struct` or `ListValue`, or an `Any`. A
level is each such message entered, the outermost at 1. Its generated decoder
takes the depth, and reads each message it holds that nests one level deeper:

    decodeNode =
        decodeNestedNode 1

    decodeNestedNode =
        Proto.Json.memo
            (\depth ->
                Proto.Json.message
                    (Json.Decode.succeed Node
                        |> Proto.Json.messageField "children" [] (Json.Decode.list (Json.Decode.map Node_ (Json.Decode.lazy (\_ -> decodeNestedNode (depth + 1)))))
                    )
            )

A message on a cycle makes its decoder for each depth once, with
[`memo`](#memo), as above. A message on no cycle, whose depth the schema
bounds, has a function of the depth instead, and the message holding it reads
it with [`deeperWith`](#deeperWith). A message that holds an `Any` takes the
registry, so its decoder at a depth is built as the value is read, with
[`deeperWith`](#deeperWith) on a cycle and [`deeper`](#deeper) off one. Past
the limit a decoder fails with "Expecting messages nested at most 100 deep".

@docs memo, deeper, deeperWith


